lemma_148_vector_bundle_extension.proof:
{
  "lemma_id": "lemma_148_vector_bundle_extension",
  "cited_lemmas": [],
  "cited_references": [],
  "proof": "Let j: V -> U be the open immersion and put G = j_*E. Since E is locally free of finite rank, E is quasi-coherent on V. Pushforward along an open immersion preserves quasi-coherence (on an affine open of U the corresponding module is the module of sections over the intersection with V, and restriction to smaller principal opens is localization), so G is quasi-coherent on U. The canonical map E -> j^*G is an isomorphism, because for x in V the stalk (G)_x is E_x. After identifying E with its image, we have G|_V = E. Step 1 (affine lemma). Let X = Spec A be a noetherian affine scheme, let W be an open subset of X, let Q be a quasi-coherent sheaf on X, and assume that Q|_W is coherent. Then there exists a coherent subsheaf L of Q on X such that L|_W = Q|_W. Indeed, W is quasi-compact, so we may choose finitely many principal opens D(f_1), ..., D(f_m) contained in W whose union is W. Put M = Gamma(X, Q). For each a, Q(D(f_a)) = M_{f_a}, and coherence of Q|_W says that M_{f_a} is a finitely generated A_{f_a}-module. Choose generators h_{a,1

}, ..., h_{a,r_a} of M_{f_a} over A_{f_a}. Write each h_{a,b} = m_{a,b}/f_a^{k_{a,b}} with m_{a,b} in M and k_{a,b} >= 0. Let N be the A-submodule of M generated by all the elements m_{a,b}. The module N is finitely generated over A, and because A is noetherian the quasi-coherent sheaf L associated to N is coherent. Since N is a submodule of M, L is a subsheaf of Q. For a fixed a, N_{f_a} contains m_{a,b}/1 for every b; since f_a is invertible in A_{f_a}, it also contains h_{a,b} = m_{a,b}/f_a^{k_{a,b}}. Therefore N_{f_a} contains the A_{f_a}-submodule of M_{f_a} generated by the h_{a,b}, which is M_{f_a}. The opposite inclusion is immediate, so N_{f_a} = M_{f_a}. Hence L(D(f_a)) = Q(D(f_a)) for every a, and because the D(f_a) cover W, L|_W = Q|_W. This proves the affine lemma. Step 2. Since U is noetherian, it admits a finite affine open cover U = U_1 union ... union U_n. Put V_i = V cap U_i. For each i, apply the affine lemma to X = U_i, W = V_i, and Q = G|_{U_i}. The sheaf G|_{V_i} is E|_{V_i}, which is coherent because E is locally free of finite rank. Thus we obtain a coherent subsheaf F_i of G|_{U_i} such that F_i|_{V_i} = E|_{V_i}. Step 3. For each i, let i_i: U_i -> U be the open immersion. The quotient G|_{U_i}/F_i is quasi-coherent on U_i, and its pushforward i_{i*}(G|_{U_i}/F_i) is quasi-coherent on U. The natural restriction morphism G -> i_{i*}(G|_{U_i}) is a morphism of quasi-coherent sheaves; composing it with the quotient map G|_{U_i} -> G|_{U_i}/F_i gives a morphism phi_i: G -> i_{i*}(G|_{U_i}/F_i) of quasi-coherent sheaves. Define H_i = ker(phi_i). The kernel of a morphism of quasi-coherent sheaves is quasi-coherent, so H_i is a quasi-coherent subsheaf of G. For any open W subset U, H_i(W) is the set of sections s in G(W) whose restriction to W cap U_i belongs to F_i(W cap U_i). In particular, H_i|_{U_i} = F_i, because on U_i the morphism phi_i is just the quotient map G|_{U_i} -> G|_{U_i}/F_i. Now define F = intersection over i H_i, equivalently F = ker(G -> direct sum_i G/H_i). The quotient G/H_i is quasi-coherent, and the kernel of a morphism of quasi-coherent sheaves is quasi-coherent, so F is a quasi-coherent subsheaf of G. Step 4. We prove that F|_V = E. Let W be an open subset of V. Since G|_V = E, we have G(W) = E(W). If s in E(W), then for each i the restriction s|_{W cap U_i} lies in E(W cap U_i) = F_i(W cap U_i); hence s in H_i(W) for every i, so s in F(W). Thus E(W) subset F(W). The reverse inclusion F(W) subset G(W) = E(W) is immediate. Therefore F|_V = E. Step 5. It remains to show that F is coherent. For each i, F|_{U_i} is a subsheaf of H_i|_{U_i} = F_i. Write U_i = Spec A_i. The sheaf F_i corresponds to a finitely generated A_i-module N_i, and F|_{U_i} is quasi-coherent, so it corresponds to an A_i-submodule of N_i. Since A_i is noetherian, N_i is a noetherian A_i-module, and every A_i-submodule of N_i is finitely generated. Hence F|_{U_i} is coherent on U_i. Coherence is local on the target, and the U_i cover U, so F is coherent on U. This completes the proof. Note that regularity of U was not used; noetherianity suffices."
}
